\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Convergence of the Rosseland mean opacities vs. the frequency resolution in $\Delta \nu /\nu $. Top: iron for Type~Ia-like conditions, $\rho = 1 \times 10^{-13}$~g/cm$^3$, $T = 1$~eV, $t = 20$~days. Bottom: erbium (lanthanide, $Z = 68$); $\rho = 1 \times 10^{-12}$~g/cm$^3$, $T = 1$~eV, $t = 1$~day. In each panel the blue solid curve is the ordinary Rosseland mean $\kappa _R$ of the saturated expansion opacity, and the orange dashed curve is the effective expansion Rosseland mean $\kappa _R^{\rm  exp}$.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:convergence}{{2}{3}{Convergence of the Rosseland mean opacities vs. the frequency resolution in $\Delta \nu /\nu $. Top: iron for Type~Ia-like conditions, $\rho = 1 \times 10^{-13}$~g/cm$^3$, $T = 1$~eV, $t = 20$~days. Bottom: erbium (lanthanide, $Z = 68$); $\rho = 1 \times 10^{-12}$~g/cm$^3$, $T = 1$~eV, $t = 1$~day. In each panel the blue solid curve is the ordinary Rosseland mean $\kappa _R$ of the saturated expansion opacity, and the orange dashed curve is the effective expansion Rosseland mean $\kappa _R^{\rm exp}$}{figure.2}{}}
